CANDIDATE root cause of mode 'P' READ hang (3/3 byte-identical
across 4 bitstreams).  See "POST-HW TEST" section below — this
turned out to be PARTIAL, not the full root cause.

Found by reading neorv32_demo.map.rpt "Registers Removed During
Synthesis" section after patch 0011 (NH6 reset stretcher) failed
to change the trace:

  wb_altasmi_parallel|epcs_ctrl|...|end_read_reg
      ; Stuck at GND due to stuck port data_in
  wb_altasmi_parallel|epcs_ctrl|...|add_rollover_reg
      ; Lost fanout
  wb_altasmi_parallel|epcs_ctrl|...|read_add_cntr|counter_reg_bit[0..24]
      ; Lost fanout

Three closely-related megafunction-internal registers were optimized
away by Quartus:
  - read_add_cntr: 25-bit READ-address counter
  - add_rollover_reg: end-of-address detector
  - end_read_reg: signals READ FSM completion (→ deasserts busy_wire)

The pruning cascade started from the wrapper's
`.read_address ()` empty connection on line 91.  Quartus saw "no
one reads read_address" → pruned read_add_cntr (lost fanout) →
add_rollover_reg lost fanout → end_read_reg.data_in becomes
constant 0 → end_read_reg stuck at GND → optimized away.  Net
effect: the altasmi READ state machine has no completion detector,
so busy_wire stays high forever after a READ opcode is issued.

This matches every empirical observation:
- Phase A RDSR works: uses a different megafunction internal FSM
  (read_status_reg path) that is not affected by the prune.
- Phase B READ hangs at pre-amble: read_add_cntr is gone, FSM
  cannot advance past pre-amble.
- 3/3 byte-identical capture (md5 d9330cae...) across pre-NH1,
  NH1, diagnostic, and NH6-stretcher bitstreams: the qmegawiz
  output epcs_ctrl.v is identical in all four; pruning is
  deterministic.
- The NH6 reset stretcher had zero effect: the missing registers
  cannot be brought back by a reset; they're not in the bitstream.

Fix: connect .read_address(am_read_addr) and surface am_read_addr
over MMIO 0x18 (RADDR register).  The MMIO read prevents the
"lost fanout" cascade.  Secondary benefit: runtime diagnostic of
READ progress — firmware can now poll read_address to see how
far the megafunction has advanced.

Phase 3 closure criterion (pre-HW expectation): with this fix,
map.rpt would show read_add_cntr, add_rollover_reg, end_read_reg
NOT in the "Registers Removed During Synthesis" list, and HW
test would show Phase B READ completing.

POST-HW TEST PARTIAL — REFUTED AS SOLE FIX (2026-05-17 evening,
bitstream md5 011a4c374764cb32a500495f52206430): map.rpt shows
read_add_cntr fully restored (24 regs, 49 LEs — visible at
hierarchy `epcs_ctrl_altasmi_parallel_0ot2_component|lpm_counter:
read_add_cntr`) BUT end_read_reg + add_rollover_reg + counter
bit[24] are STILL pruned ("Stuck at GND" / "Lost fanout").
Connecting read_address[23:0] surfaces the 24-bit address counter
but the 25th rollover bit and the end-of-read register prune from
a separate path — the hardwired `.rden(1'b1)` on line 78 of
wb_altasmi_parallel.v.

HW trace (docs/captures/diag_baseline_hang.log, md5 d9330cae):
byte-identical to NH6 result.  Phase B still hangs at STATUS=0x03.

Actual root cause was identified in the next patch (NH8):
end_read_reg.data_in = (~rden) & do_read & data_valid_wire &
end_read_byte — with rden hardwired to 1, ~rden is constant 0,
end_read_reg input is permanently 0, FSM cannot fire end-of-read,
stage4 deadlock per epcs_ctrl.v line 377 FSM clk_en gate.

Patch retained as DEFENSIVE: read_add_cntr restoration is
spec-correct (without it, multi-byte reads cannot track address
progression even if the FSM deadlock is otherwise fixed).  Also
gives runtime address sniff via MMIO 0x18.  No revert.

Patch 0011 (NH6 reset stretcher) also retained as defensive.
---
 rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
index 97f1bfb..59a8834 100644
--- a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
+++ b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
@@ -28,6 +28,11 @@
 //                       b3=illegal_write_sticky (clears on reset)
 //   0x10  DATAOUT (R)   [7:0]    latched altasmi.dataout (reading clears DV)
 //   0x14  ESTAT   (R)   [7:0]    altasmi.status_out (latest read_status result)
+//   0x18  RADDR   (R)   [23:0]   altasmi.read_address (live read-address
+//                                counter; primary purpose: prevent
+//                                synthesis pruning of read_add_cntr →
+//                                end_read_reg; secondary purpose: runtime
+//                                diagnostic of READ progress)
 
 module wb_altasmi_parallel (
     input         clk,
@@ -66,6 +71,9 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
     wire [7:0]  am_status_out;
     wire        am_illegal_erase;
     wire        am_illegal_write;
+    wire [23:0] am_read_addr;       // NH7 fix: must be connected so Quartus
+                                    // doesn't prune read_add_cntr →
+                                    // end_read_reg.  Surfaced over MMIO 0x18.
 
     // ── Power-on reset stretcher (NH6 fix) ────────────────────────────
     // altasmi_parallel spec: reset must be asserted ≥250 ns at power-up
@@ -108,7 +116,7 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
         .status_out    (am_status_out),
         .illegal_erase (am_illegal_erase),
         .illegal_write (am_illegal_write),
-        .read_address  ()  // diagnostic-only; not surfaced over MMIO
+        .read_address  (am_read_addr)
     );
 
     // ── Sticky flags + dataout latch ──────────────────────────────────
@@ -194,6 +202,7 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
                             dataout_read_req <= 1'b1;
                         end
                         8'h14: xbus_dat_r <= {24'd0, am_status_out};
+                        8'h18: xbus_dat_r <= {8'd0, am_read_addr};
                         default: xbus_dat_r <= 32'd0;
                     endcase
                 end
